\chapter{Building Programs, Building Environments}
\label{ch:development}
\index{compilers}\index{yacc}\index{lex}\index{make}\index{development tools}

\section{The toolchain is part of the system}

Unix offered a place to write programs, not merely a place to execute
finished applications. An editor, compiler, assembler, linker, debugger,
and manual formed a practical sequence from idea to running code.
Their boundaries made it possible to replace one stage or inspect an
intermediate result. A compiler error and a linker error could be located
in different parts of the construction process.

This environment also sustained itself. The system's programs were built
on the system, and its documentation was prepared with its document tools.
The 1974 paper calls attention to this self-supporting quality.
It mattered institutionally: improvements to the tools immediately
improved the developers' ability to improve the system
\parencite[sec. 1]{ritchie-thompson}.

C benefited from that environment, but Unix was never exclusively a
C-language world. Fortran supported numerical work; assemblers remained
necessary; interpreters and little languages served specialized tasks.
Later Lisp, ML-family languages, scripting languages, and managed runtimes
found homes there. A process interface based on arguments, descriptors,
and status does not require all participants to share a source language.

\section{Generators turn descriptions into code}

Stephen Johnson's yacc generated parsers from a grammar and associated
actions. Lex, associated with Michael Lesk and Eric Schmidt, generated
lexical analyzers from pattern descriptions. The pairing made language
implementation more accessible by expressing recurring machinery as
specifications rather than rewriting every state transition by hand.

A lexical analyzer recognizes tokens; a parser relates those tokens to
a grammar. The distinction helps authors separate character-level details
from structural rules. It does not eliminate ambiguity or semantic
analysis. A grammar can be ambiguous, an action can have a bug, and a
perfectly parsed program can still be meaningless.

Generators also create a maintenance obligation. The generated file may
be difficult to review directly; the grammar and generator version become
essential inputs. Checking in generated output can make bootstrapping
easier while introducing the risk that source and output diverge.
A build needs a declared rule for which representation is authoritative.

These tools belong to the same family of ideas as eqn and tbl.
Each lets an author work in a representation closer to the problem,
then translates it into a lower-level mechanism. The common principle is
not that every problem needs a new language. It is that a suitable
representation can remove repetitive reasoning from ordinary code.

\section{Make records relationships}

Stuart Feldman's make addressed the repeated work of rebuilding programs.
A project contains targets and prerequisites. If an input has changed,
the relevant target and its dependents need rebuilding. Encoding these
relationships lets the tool decide what work is necessary.

A small original example is enough to show the model:

\begin{verbatim}
report: report.o parse.o
        cc -o report report.o parse.o

report.o: report.c parse.h
        cc -c report.c

parse.o: parse.c parse.h
        cc -c parse.c
\end{verbatim}

In an actual makefile, each recipe begins with a tab. The example's
important content is the dependency graph, including the header.
If the header is omitted, the build may succeed while leaving an object
file compiled against an obsolete declaration. Incremental correctness
depends on complete inputs, not only on convenient command notation.

Traditional timestamp-based decisions are economical, but they have
limits. Generated inputs, clock differences, hidden environment changes,
and tools whose versions are not recorded can produce stale or
nonreproducible output. Modern build systems use techniques such as
content hashing, declared toolchains, sandboxed actions, and remote caches.
These are extensions of the desire to make the construction process
explicit.

A document build has the same structure. Citations require bibliography
processing; references may require reruns; an index has its own processor.
A single successful typesetting pass is not necessarily a finished book.
The build system in this repository delegates that dependency work to
latexmk rather than expecting the reader to remember an invocation sequence.

\section{Debugging depends on observable state}
\index{debuggers}\index{tracing}

A debugger connects machine behavior to source-level expectations.
It can stop execution, inspect variables, examine a stack, and report
where a failure occurred. Optimized code complicates that relationship:
a variable can disappear, instructions can move, and the stack may not
resemble a simple sequence of source calls.

Tracing offers a complementary view. A system-call trace can show which
files a program opens, where it blocks, and what the kernel reports.
It can reveal a missing configuration file or a permission error without
understanding every internal function. It cannot by itself explain all
user-space computation or prove that a program's result is correct.

Profiling asks yet another question: where does time or another resource
go? Sampling, counters, and instrumented events expose different aspects
of behavior. Measurement overhead and attribution rules matter.
A program waiting for a remote service may consume little CPU while
remaining painfully slow for its user.

Unix's preference for inspectable files and separate processes often makes
such tools convenient, but observability is not automatic.
A program must preserve useful error context, avoid corrupting data
streams with diagnostics, and give administrators enough information to
connect a failure with its cause. Silence on ordinary success is compatible
with detailed evidence when something goes wrong.

\section{Versions make collaboration historical}
\index{SCCS}\index{RCS}\index{Git}\index{version control}

SCCS and RCS supported source history before the distributed systems now
familiar to many programmers. Later systems expanded collaboration across
repositories and institutions. Git, developed in 2005 in the setting of
Linux kernel development, made content-addressed objects and distributed
history central to a widely adopted workflow.

A version-control system preserves relationships among states.
It can show what changed, associate a change with an explanation, and
support branching and merging. It cannot supply a good explanation if
the author records only ``fix.'' Nor can a clean merge prove semantic
compatibility: two edits can apply without conflict and still violate a
shared invariant.

History also supports investigation. A regression can be narrowed by
searching for the change that introduced it. An old implementation can
be compared with a later one. Spinellis's reconstruction extends this
practice to periods before the relevant systems used a continuous modern
repository, while warning readers not to mistake reconstructed metadata
for an untouched contemporaneous record \parencite{spinellis}.

The repository is thus both a collaboration mechanism and an argument
about provenance. Source history is especially valuable when accompanied
by build instructions and tests that make older states interpretable.
Without those, preservation can become a collection of files whose
behavior nobody can reproduce.

\section{Languages beyond C}
\index{ML}\index{OCaml}\index{Standard ML}

The supplied collection includes Unix programming treatments in Standard
ML and OCaml. Their presence is a useful corrective to histories that
identify Unix programming with a single syntax. File descriptors,
processes, and sockets can be exposed through different type systems and
runtime models. The operating-system contract survives translation into
a language's abstractions.

A managed language may wrap a descriptor in an object with buffering,
exceptions, or automatic cleanup. Those features can make programs safer,
but they do not repeal the underlying semantics. Garbage collection does
not guarantee timely release of a scarce descriptor. A buffered channel
can retain data the kernel has not yet seen. An exception can interrupt
a multi-step protocol before cleanup finishes.

Type systems can express distinctions that C convention leaves implicit.
They may help separate bytes from text, optional values from present ones,
and valid states from invalid combinations. At foreign-function boundaries,
those guarantees need careful preservation. An unchecked C interface can
reintroduce lifetime and representation errors beneath a safe wrapper.

The larger lesson is that Unix supplies useful primitive agreements,
while languages can improve how programmers handle them. The right
abstraction can expose an important failure rather than hide it.
A function returning a result that must be checked is often more helpful
than a convenient wrapper that silently discards the distinction.

\section{A development environment is an accumulated promise}

Over decades, Unix environments grew from a small local toolkit into
platforms hosting enormous language ecosystems. Package registries,
generated code, native libraries, compiler versions, and deployment
runtimes added dependencies far beyond a handful of C files.
The task of understanding a build became correspondingly harder.

The original strengths remain useful when applied deliberately.
Record inputs. Keep stages inspectable. Distinguish generated artifacts
from their sources. Make failure visible. Preserve enough history to
explain why a choice was made. These practices do not depend on a
particular shell or compiler.

They also connect programming with the subject of the next chapter.
Raymond's account of the Unix tradition turns many such practices into
explicit design advice. The advice is most useful when tested against
concrete programs and against the objections of users who have paid for
its failures.
